.TH EUCLID-CLI-ESM-ARCHIVE-OBJECT 1 "October 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-esm-archive-object, restore-object \- move an object to the archive, and back
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli esm archive-object
.RB ( \-\-bucket | \-b
.I NAME|ERN
.BR \-\-key | \-k
.I KEY
|
.BR \-\-ern | \-e
.IR ERN )
.br
.B euclid-cli esm restore-object
.RB ( \-\-bucket | \-b
.I NAME|ERN
.BR \-\-key | \-k
.I KEY
|
.BR \-\-ern | \-e
.IR ERN )
.RB [ \-\-days | \-d
.IR N ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B archive-object
archives one object now, whatever the bucket's lifecycle rules say: its bytes
are appended to the bucket's zip for the month in
.BR euclid.modules.esm.archive-dir ,
and reading, downloading, copying or moving it is refused with 409 until it is
restored. Listing it, its size and attributes, and deleting it work as before.
Only a complete object can be archived, and not one in an internal bucket.
.PP
.B restore-object
extracts an archived object's bytes from its zip, checked against their size and
CRC, and makes it readable again. It stays
online for
.BR \-\-days ,
or else the restore days of the rule that covers it, or else
.B euclid.modules.esm.restore-days
(7). When that grace period is over and nobody has read it, the lifecycle sweep
archives it again; one that was read is held to its rule from that read.
.PP
Both answer the object's lifecycle state, with
.BR archiveFile ,
.BR archivedAt ,
.B restoredAt
and
.B restoredUntil
where they apply, and both are idempotent: an object already where it was asked
to go is answered as it is.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-bucket " " \fINAME|ERN\fR
The object's bucket, by name or ERN. With
.BR \-\-key .
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-key " " \fIKEY\fR
The object's key within the bucket.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-ern " " \fIERN\fR
The object's ERN, instead of
.B \-\-bucket
and
.BR \-\-key .
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-days " " \fIN\fR
restore-object only: days to keep it online, from 1 to 36500.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli esm archive-object -b incoming -k 2024/onix.xml
euclid-cli esm restore-object -b incoming -k 2024/onix.xml --days 3
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The object is in the state asked for.
.TP
.B 1
No such object, one that cannot be archived, a file that could not be moved,
not permitted, or invalid arguments.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-esm-set-bucket-lifecycle (1),
.BR euclid-cli-esm (1)
